P(X = k) = \binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}
\] Here, \(n = 6\), \(k = 3\), and \(p = \frac{1}{2}\). So the probability is: \[
P(X = 3) = \binom{6}{3} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^3 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^3 = \binom{6}{3} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^6 = \frac{6!}{3!3!} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^6 = 20 \times \frac{1}{64} = \frac{20}{64} = \frac{5}{16}
\] The answer is: <answer> \(\frac{5}{16}\) </answer>

